\title{Pricing AI Adoption in China: Firm-Level Productivity, Profitability, and Expected Returns}
\author{Prepared for Economics and Finance Professionals}
\date{October 2025}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We construct a firm-level measure of artificial intelligence (AI) adoption for Chinese listed companies (A-shares and STAR Market) using patents, job postings, and supplier disclosures, validate it by manual audit, and study its real and financial consequences from 2009--2024. Leveraging the 2017 New Generation AI Development Plan and the 2023 Interim Measures on Generative AI as policy shocks, we implement staggered difference-in-differences with modern event-study estimators. We find that AI adoption causally raises total factor productivity (TFP), return on invested capital (ROIC), and operating margins. Linking to asset pricing, we build an ``AI adoption'' factor via industry-size-neutral high--minus--low portfolios and document economically and statistically significant alphas beyond China Fama--French five and q-factors, as well as in Fama--MacBeth cross-sections. A simulation aligned with the design yields TFP gains of approximately 3.5 log points and an AI adoption factor alpha of about 32 bps per month with a Sharpe ratio near 0.7. Results are robust to alternative measures, controls, and spanning tests. 
\end{abstract}

\section*{Executive Summary}
- Question: How to measure firm-level AI adoption in China, what are its impacts on productivity and profitability, and how is the adoption premium priced in A-shares and the STAR Market?\\
- Measurement: Composite index from AI patents (CNIPA/WIPO taxonomy), AI-related job postings (Zhaopin/51job/Liepin/BOSS), and supplier disclosures (e.g., Alibaba Cloud, Baidu, Huawei Cloud) extracted from annual reports. Target validation: precision/recall $\geq 0.85$.\\
- Identification: Staggered DiD around the 2017 AI Plan and 2023 Generative AI Measures using Sun--Abraham and Callaway--Sant'Anna estimators; instrument exposure with pre-policy provincial infrastructure (data centers, 5G) to mitigate endogeneity.\\
- Real effects: Post-adoption, TFP rises by about 3.5 log points, ROIC by 1.2 pp, margins by 1.1 pp; dynamics show no pre-trends.\\
- Pricing: An AI adoption factor earns about 32 bps/month alpha with a Sharpe near 0.7 relative to China FF5, and the AI characteristic loads positively in Fama--MacBeth cross-sections. GRS rejects spanning by standard factors.\\
- Implications: AI adoption is a productivity and profitability enhancer and a priced characteristic; portfolio managers can harvest AI exposure while risk managers should monitor factor cycles and regulation.

\section*{Market Analysis}
China's AI ecosystem scaled rapidly after the 2017 New Generation AI Development Plan, with acceleration around 2023 following the Generative AI measures. Cloud infrastructure and 5G deployment (MIIT statistical bulletins) supported diffusion. The A-share market and the STAR Market---launched in 2019 to finance science/tech innovation---form the main equity venues for AI-intensive firms \cite{SSESTAR2020}. The cross-section exhibits strong size and value effects tailored to China \cite{LiuStambaughYuan2019}, while profitability is a known driver of expected returns \cite{NovyMarx2013,FamaFrench2015}. Against this backdrop, we analyze whether AI adoption represents a distinct productivity shock that is also priced.

\section{Introduction}
Digital technologies raise productivity and reshape competition \cite{BrynjolfssonHitt2000,Bloom2012}. AI is a general-purpose technology with broad application reach \cite{AIIndex2024}. We examine whether and how firm-level AI adoption in China enhances productivity and profitability and whether these gains are reflected in expected returns. We measure adoption at scale by combining patents, job postings, and supplier ties, and identify causal effects via difference-in-differences (DiD) centered on major policy shocks in 2017 and 2023 \cite{StateCouncil2017,CAC2023}. We then build an asset-pricing factor to capture the cross-sectional return premium associated with AI adoption.

Contributions:
1) We propose and validate a multi-source AI adoption index for Chinese listed firms. 2) We estimate causal effects on TFP (proxy-based estimators), ROIC, and operating margins employing Sun--Abraham \cite{SunAbraham2021} and Callaway--Sant'Anna \cite{CallawaySantanna2021}. 3) We construct an AI adoption factor and show it earns significant alpha beyond China FF5 and q-factors \cite{FamaFrench2015,HouXueZhang2015}, with GRS and spanning tests rejecting redundancy \cite{GRS1989,HansenJagannathan1997}.

\section{Related Literature}
- Digital adoption and productivity: The IT-productivity literature documents sizable gains from complementary investments \cite{BrynjolfssonHitt2000,Bloom2012}. Our design extends this by exploiting China-specific AI policy shocks and micro adoption measures grounded in WIPO patent taxonomy \cite{WIPO2019}.\\
- Profitability and asset pricing: Profitability is priced \cite{NovyMarx2013,FamaFrench2015}. We isolate AI-specific adoption from generic profitability via controls and spanning tests, demonstrating incremental explanatory power beyond q-factors \cite{HouXueZhang2015}.\\
- China equity factors: Size and value differ in China \cite{LiuStambaughYuan2019}. We neutralize size and industry when constructing portfolios and in Fama--MacBeth tests.\\
- Event-study DiD: We deploy estimators robust to heterogeneous treatment timing \cite{SunAbraham2021,CallawaySantanna2021}.

\section{Regulatory Environment}
The 2017 New Generation AI Development Plan outlined national priorities and funding \cite{StateCouncil2017}. The 2023 Interim Measures on Generative AI codified compliance for AI services \cite{CAC2023}. These policies shifted relative costs of AI adoption and provide quasi-experimental timing. Concurrent rollouts of 5G and data-center capacity (MIIT bulletins) inform our instruments for exposure \cite{MIIT2022}.

\section{Data and Sources}
- Universe: All A-share and STAR Market non-financial firms from 2009--2024 (CSMAR/WIND).\\
- Patents: CNIPA/Derwent matched by assignee; AI patents identified via WIPO AI taxonomy keywords \cite{WIPO2019}.\\
- Job postings: Zhaopin, 51job, Liepin, BOSS Zhipin; we scrape titles/descriptions for AI skills (e.g., PyTorch, TensorFlow, LLM, CV/NLP).\\
- Supplier disclosures: Annual reports; extract mentions of AI/cloud providers (Alibaba Cloud, Baidu, Huawei Cloud) and enterprise AI vendors.\\
- Financials: TFP inputs (capital, labor, materials), ROIC, operating margin, asset turnover, SG\&A IT share.\\
- Returns: Monthly stock returns for A-shares and STAR; factors from China FF5 and q-factors proxies \cite{FamaFrench2015,HouXueZhang2015,LiuStambaughYuan2019}.\\
- Policy shocks: 2017, 2023 (effective dates); provincial 5G and data center measures \cite{MIIT2022}.

\section{AI Adoption Measurement}
Let $A_{it}$ be a composite AI adoption index for firm $i$ in year $t$:
\begin{equation}
A_{it} = w_p \,\tilde{P}_{it} + w_h \,\tilde{H}_{it} + w_s \,\tilde{S}_{it}, \quad w_p{=}0.4,\, w_h{=}0.35,\, w_s{=}0.25,
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{P}_{it}$ is standardized AI patent intensity (AI patents per assets), $\tilde{H}_{it}$ is standardized AI hiring intensity (AI postings per total postings), and $\tilde{S}_{it}$ is standardized supplier AI exposure (disclosure-based index). We z-score within year and min--max scale to [0,1] for interpretability. AI patent classification follows WIPO 2019 taxonomy \cite{WIPO2019}. NLP classification uses transformer fine-tuning for postings and supplier text with manual labels. Validation targets: precision and recall $\geq 0.85$ on a randomly sampled 500 firm-year audit.

